The decrease of the hydrodynamic diameter and increased in hydrodynamic diameter distribution after 10 wt.% of CCS can be explained by the formation of new particles stabilized by the CCS oligomers formed in the aqueous phase, that act as emulsifier to stabilize the polymer particles [8,20]. At the beginning of the polymerization, the reaction mixture was composed of low average hydrodynamic diameters (≈100 nm) and had a bluish aspect. During the monomer mixture (BA and MMA) and HPO feed stages, the amount of oligomeric CCS or CCS-free in the water phase acted as an emulsifier and new particles were formed by homogeneous or coagulative nucleation, which suggests that different mechanisms of nucleation are in effect. Stable polymer latexes were produced in the absence of surfactant due the graft starch polymers located at the water/particle interface [20,23]. In general, graft polymerizations occur by hydrogen abstraction of the C–H on the starch backbone or from the hydroxyl groups [7,20,23], leading to the formation of macro-radicals (CCS

                        ). These radicals can also directly initiate polymerization, resulting in the formation of polymer nanoparticles in addition to graft polymer nanoparticles [20–23]. These active macro-radicals can be also terminated by chain transfer, coupling or disproportionation reactions between CCS

                         macro-radicals and primary or monomeric radicals [23]. The viscosity of the control sample (CCS-0) (Table 3) and that of the fresh latex CCS-7 were the same, at around 3.2 mPa·s, while the addition of 15 wt.% of CCS resulted in a significant increase in viscosity (22 mPa·s).

The 1H NMR spectra of CCS, CCS-0 and CCS-10 are shown in Fig. 5. From inspection of the 1H NMR spectra, we see evidence that MMA and BA are grafted onto CCS. As expected, the signals between 4.6 and 5.5 ppm were assigned to the hydrogen atoms of the starch backbone, related to the glycoside group, and the peak at 3.6 ppm was attributed to OH protons linked to CCS [23,35]. The signal at 2.5 ppm was attributed to hydrogen atoms of the solvent DMSO. In the case of CCS-10 (Fig. 5c) the signals at 1.0 (−CH2–), 1.25 and 0.87 (−CH3) ppm indicate the presence of hydrocarbon side groups on the starch backbone. The presence of the signal at 3.1 ppm is assigned to hydrogen atoms bonded to a nitrogen atom, confirming the presence of CCS in the backbone of the graft polymer (Fig. 5c), and ascribed to the +NH3Cl− cationic substituting group [23,35]. The presence of the signals between 4.6–5.5 ppm also confirm the presence of CCS on the backbone by radical polymerization. When comparing the spectra for CCS-0 and CCS-10, the CCS-10 spectrum in Fig. 5(b) shows peaks specific to poly(BA/MMA-g-CCS) compared with the CCS-0 peaks. The results of 1H NMR analysis were in agreement with the results of grafting percentage (Table 4
                        ) and FT-IR analysis (Fig. 6
                        ), confirming that the polymers have been successfully grafted onto CCS.

The effect of CCS concentration on the graft percentage (GP) is shown in Table 4. The GP values were obtained after 20 h in chloroform by Soxhlet extraction. The results indicate that the amount of hydrolyzed CCS shows a significant effect on the GP. The graft percentage decreases continuously with an increase in CCS concentration, indicating a greater grafting efficiency with low amounts of CCS. The polymer CCS-7 prepared with the lowest content of CCS (7.0 wt.%) showed the highest graft percentage (283 %), while the polymer CCS-20 synthesized with the highest amount of CCS (20 wt.%) showed the lowest graft percentage (85 %). The decrease in GP can be explained based on an increase in macro-radicals (CCS

                        ) concentration and the ratio monomer/CCS employed [18,23]. These active macro-radicals in excess, can initiated the polymerization of MMA and BA to form homopolymer or terminate by chain transfer, coupling or disproportionation reactions between CCS

                         macro-radicals and primary or monomeric radicals. In both situations the GP decrease and coagulum content increase (Table 4). The changes in GP may be strongly correlated with the presence of CCS on the surface of the particles and free in the water phase [22,23].
